  "chunk_cell_count": 50,
  "description": "The 397-cell NASA POWER 0.5-degree analysis lattice (N51/W-125/S31/E-104) covered with ERA5-Land daily shortwave radiation, read through the keyless Open-Meteo archive. This is a SECOND upstream for a signal NASA POWER already produces, not a new signal: NASA POWER's ALLSKY_SFC_SW_DWN lane is complete at 397/397 cells but permanently capped at 2026-05-31 by that parameter's ~2-month publication lag, while the Open-Meteo ERA5-Land archive publishes the same daily quantity with roughly six days of lag. Both series carry signal_name surface_shortwave_radiation in normalized_unit MJ/m^2/day and NO conversion is applied: ALLSKY_SFC_SW_DWN and shortwave_radiation_sum are both a daily sum of megajoules per square metre (verified against Open-Meteo's own daily-variable table, which publishes MJ/m2, and against the 1,166,676 stored NASA rows whose mean of 16.65 is dimensionally MJ/m^2/day and not a kWh/m^2/day series). The lattice is the NASA one rather than this lane's usual sentinel2-ndvi-0p25deg lattice so that this signal's coverage definition stays exactly what NASA established for it, and so that geo.climate_field_observation's 0.5-degree grid predicate can still address the rows. support_key stays era5-land-0.1deg, which is this lane's honest SPATIAL support and is what lets a reader tell a 0.1-degree ERA5-Land sample from a 0.5-degree NASA POWER sample of the same cell; the serving surfaces that currently gate on support_key='surface' and source.key='nasa-power-daily' must be widened in a separate reviewed change before these rows reach a map. CONTINUATION 2026-08-08: HistoricalBackfillWindow requires an exact four-calendar-year span, so this plan's window necessarily overlaps the NASA lane's own days under a different source_release_id; uq_signal_observation_release_cell_signal_time includes source_release_id by design and downstream covariate reads already DISTINCT ON (signal_name, day) ORDER BY data_available_at DESC (see plans/boise-trekker-rim-covariate-layer-and-first-model-2026-08-02.md). The ground this plan adds over the NASA lane is the roughly 63 days from 2026-06-01 to the window end; window end_date is the upstream frontier measured for this lane on 2026-08-08 and shared byte-for-byte with the three sibling continuation plans, not a guess.",
  "grid_name": "nasa-power-0.5-degree",
  "grid_resolution_m": 55660,
  "model": "era5_land",
  "native_grid_degrees": 0.1,
  "native_grid_name": "era5-land-0.1-degree",
  "native_grid_resolution_m": 9000,
  "parameters": [
    "shortwave_radiation_sum"
  ],
  "release_set_as_of": "2026-08-16T23:59:59Z",
